NUM-60: correct a bottom orifice below tailwater for submergence once

When the tailwater stands above a bottom orifice's crest, the orifice's
head is already the head difference h1 - h2, both to decide whether it runs
in its weir regime and in the weir formula. StructureSolver::
computeOrificeFlowK then also applied Villemonte's submergence factor, which
assumes the head was measured from the crest (as legacy link.c does). The
flow was too small in the weir regime and jumped by the inverse of the
factor where the weir and orifice formulas meet (H*), so an inflow inside
the jump had no steady state. Apply the factor to side orifices only. Side
orifices, bottom orifices with the tailwater below the crest, and every
orifice running full are unchanged.

Apply from the root of a Stormwater-Management-Model checkout at cb3e192b:
    git apply NUM-60_swmm600.patch

diff --git a/src/engine/hydraulics/HydStructures.cpp b/src/engine/hydraulics/HydStructures.cpp
index 77d61bf..036eb08 100644
--- a/src/engine/hydraulics/HydStructures.cpp
+++ b/src/engine/hydraulics/HydStructures.cpp
@@ -734,7 +734,9 @@ void StructureSolver::computeOrificeFlowK(SimulationContext& ctx,
         // pow(ratio, 1.5) (ratio ≤ 1 since h1 ≥ h2 after the direction
         // swap; ratio == 1 must zero the flow, which the previous
         // `ratio < 1` guard skipped).
-        if (f < 1.0 && h2 > hcrest) {
+        // A bottom orifice below tailwater already uses the head difference
+        // h1 - h2, so it is not corrected a second time.
+        if (f < 1.0 && h2 > hcrest && is_side) {
             double ratio = (h2 - hcrest) / (h1 - hcrest);
             q *= std::pow(1.0 - std::pow(ratio, 1.5), 0.385);
         }
